"{\"issue\":\"<p>Most new films are not watchable if you are blind or Deaf. At Sundance 2026, only 16 of 90 feature films offered audio description, down from 26 in 2024. The step that decides whether a finished film reaches its full audience is a manual accessibility QC pass over the caption and audio-description files, and it is slow and expensive: manual audio-description QC runs about $9 to $14 per minute. Festivals, distributors, and streaming platforms increasingly reject non-compliant files through automated QC pipelines, so one small defect can bounce a whole delivery. And the pressure is about to become legal: ADA Title II compliance deadlines arrive in April 2027 and April 2028. No open-source tool checks both caption and audio-description conformance against WCAG 2.2, FCC, DCMP, and Netflix standards at once, so teams either pay for manual review or ship and hope.</p>\",\"solution\":\"<p id=\\\"isPasted\\\">AccessGate is the pre-check a studio, festival, or streamer runs between a finished film and its release, and to our knowledge it is the first open tool to check both accessibility surfaces at once: captions for Deaf and hard-of-hearing viewers, and audio description for blind and low-vision viewers. It scores a film's caption and audio-description files against 23 coded rules across FCC 47 CFR 79.1, WCAG 2.2, DCMP, and Netflix, and every flag cites the exact standard section it came from, retrieved at runtime from a Granite Embedding index rather than hardcoded. The caption accuracy score is NER-based with confidence bands, and it never auto-fails on ASR alone, because automatic speech recognition carries measured racial disparity (Koenecke et al., PNAS 2020), so a borderline score is flagged for human review instead of failed outright.<br><br>When a scene has a dialogue-free gap that needs audio description, the gated generative fix runs on click: watsonx-hosted Llama 3.2 Vision drafts a description sized to the silent window, a self-built DCMP structure validator checks word count, tense, voice, and overlap, Granite Guardian screens the draft for content safety, and only then does the row flip green. Nothing that fails the gate is ever accepted.<br><br>The scoring and timing engine is self-built and survives an API-deletion test: remove every hosted AI API and it still runs and still produces a report. Results export to SARIF 2.1.0 and OSCAL POA&amp;M, and the same engine backs a web app, an iOS TestFlight build, and an Android app. It was built primarily through IBM Bob on the IBM Granite stack (Granite Vision, Granite Guardian, Granite Speech, Granite Embedding) with watsonx. Bob's usage runs deep: a custom accessibility-compliance mode, a conformance rule-authoring skill, two /review audits (SARIF and OSCAL), and a self-referential MCP loop where Bob checked AccessGate's conformance output using AccessGate's own MCP tools during development.<br><br>We did not design this on assumptions about disabled audiences. We recruited a longtime audio-description user through the ACB Audio Description Project community and asked what makes audio description fail. Their answer: \\\"Really bad audio description gives me too much info, and sometimes too little info.\\\" That is exactly the axis AccessGate scores, the DCMP Description Key rule that a description must fit its silent gap, never too sparse and never overcrowded. The same user also described audio description mixed too quietly under the soundtrack, a final-mix delivery issue AccessGate does not check, since it scores the audio-description sidecar's structure, timing, and gap-fit, not the delivered mix loudness. We state that boundary openly on the live /judges page. A tool that is honest about what it does not do is one a QC team can trust with what it does.</p><p id=\\\"isPasted\\\">The same user later tested the live app with the JAWS screen reader and reported it \\\"very accessible,\\\" with every control correctly labeled. Two of their suggestions, a heading outline and a skip-to-content link, shipped the same day.</p><p>Quote used with permission, name withheld for privacy.</p><p><br></p><p>The review does not stop at a report. AccessGate opens an event-sourced review session where a QC lead accepts, dismisses with a reason, or flags each finding as a reversible, auditable operation, drivable in plain English (watsonx Granite compiles the instruction and re-grounds it to the report's real findings, so it can never act on a rule or timecode the engine did not produce), with a deterministic undo and an append-only audit trail. Findings and the gated fix also export to editor-native files (a WebVTT audio-description track, a findings CSV, WebVTT markers) that a captioner or audio-description writer opens in their own workflow.</p><p>Every finding shows its arithmetic, not just a verdict: the measured value against the limit and the delta between them, plus the exact standard clause and a link to it, so a reviewer can check the number rather than trust it.</p><p>The whole thing reproduces: a fresh clone runs 269 passing tests and the same engine that produced the demo report, CI is green on every commit, and the live /judges endpoint names which model served every generated line and which encoder retrieved every citation.</p>\"}"